					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-341213#post-1198969</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-341213/and-then-i-died-archive#post-1198969</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Mon, 11 Jul 2011 16:22:06 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Voct</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>392303</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>&#8230; what? No, that's backwards.</p> <p>You said (in your previous version of your post) &quot;I wanted to guess after the first paragraph.&quot;</p> <p>If you had guessed after the first paragraph, then you would have said &quot;807&quot;. This is the &quot;red herring&quot;. I intended for you to think it was 807. It was not 807. I deliberately wrote the text to misleadingly imply that it was 807 (an example of the literary technique known as the &quot;red herring&quot;), but in fact the hamburger was an ordinary hamburger. The reason the guy had a heart attack was that his pacemaker malfunctioned. The fact that he had been eating a wonderfully delicious hamburger mere minutes before his pacemaker malfunctioned&#8230; that was an unfortunate coincidence. The fact that he thought it was the best damn hamburger he ever ate&#8230; that was <em>not</em> because it was a hamburger that had been transmuted by SCP-807 (SCP-807 wasn't even involved); rather, it was because the hamburger was a contrast from hospital food. That's why he said &quot;maybe it's just the contrast from hospital food&quot;: because the hamburger was a contrast from hospital food. He had been in the hospital to get a pacemaker implanted; that's why he had the scar on his chest, and that's why he had gotten used to bland unsatisfying hospital food.</p> <p>When the pacemaker began to fail, this drew the attention of SCP-890 (who happened to be in the diner). Instead of performing CPR, SCP-890 sliced the guy open so as to have better access to the pacemaker; as a result, the guy died. Would he have died anyway from the defective pacemaker? Maybe, maybe not. We'll never know.</p> <p>Is that unambiguous enough for you?</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
					<item>
